3 but they are in next player's melds, so also blocked. But T1 is in the same suit as your other tiles (T6,T8,T9), so discarding T1 might help focus on Bamboo and Honors? Actually, you have tiles in all three suits, so keeping T1 maintains All Types possibility. But All Types only requires one tile of each suit and honors, which you already have even without T1 (W5, T6, B1, J1, J3). So T1 is not necessary for All Types.\n\nThus, discard B9 seems reasonable.\n\nReason: B9 is a terminal with poor connectivity, and discarding it reduces the number of suits you are trying to develop, allowing you to focus on improving the Bamboo (B1 pair) and Bamboo/Tong combinations. Keeping T1 might provide a remote chance for a Chow if you draw T2, but T2 is unseen 4, but T3 is in a meld, so it's less likely. B9 has almost no chance to form a Chow given the exposed B6-B7-B8 meld and low unseen B7/B8. Discarding B9 is the safest and most efficient move to streamline the hand toward a potential All Types or mixed hand with pungs.\n\nAnswer: Play B9"}
